1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
8 a.m. The press room of the San Francisco Examiner.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Four giant cylinders are feeding 300 pages a second to the press,

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:15,000
producing 20,000 newspapers in an hour.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
For the Examiner and the other 1,500 papers published each day in the United States.

5
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:28,000
The process begins hours earlier, at the daily news meeting,

6
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:31,000
where the executive editor meets with his department heads

7
00:00:31,001 --> 00:00:35,000
to determine what stories will run that day.

8
00:00:35,001 --> 00:00:40,000
My job is to be responsible for and oversee the paper as a whole.

9
00:00:40,001 --> 00:00:45,000
That means the news hole, the non-advertising part of the newspaper.

10
00:00:45,001 --> 00:00:49,000
It's the place where we put what it is we do every day.

11
00:00:49,001 --> 00:00:53,000
So usually that ranges in various papers.

12
00:00:53,001 --> 00:00:55,000
We like to think it's 50-50.

13
00:00:55,001 --> 00:00:59,120
So in other words, the news of the newspaper, the editorial product of the newspaper,

14
00:00:59,240 --> 00:01:01,560
gets less than 50% as compared with the ads.

15
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:08,040
News is drawn from an enormous variety of issues and events.

16
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:13,460
In order to cope with this vast and ever-changing landscape,

17
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:18,180
newspapers long ago established the tradition of assigning correspondents

18
00:01:18,181 --> 00:01:21,060
to cover regular beats like the White House.

19
00:01:21,061 --> 00:01:25,620
I'll have more to say about that at a later time.

20
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:28,380
Nothing can replace being at the White House.

21
00:01:29,220 --> 00:01:35,900
I mean, to actually be one-on-one, eyeball-to-eyeball with the President of the United States.

22
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:44,880
Today, no matter how quickly newspaper reporters react, they cannot keep pace with the flow of information.

23
00:01:44,881 --> 00:01:51,320
While a story can get its start in many different ways,

24
00:01:51,980 --> 00:01:58,560
journalists who cover international news often say their topics are now selected for them by other media.

25
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:02,000
Television is the tripwire.

26
00:02:02,001 --> 00:02:07,260
We have, I think, six or seven television sets in the newsroom.

27
00:02:08,060 --> 00:02:11,660
They're tuned to CNN, to C-SPAN.

28
00:02:11,661 --> 00:02:17,980
CNN really is a realization of a global village.

29
00:02:18,300 --> 00:02:24,140
That when you sit here and watch bombs falling in Iraq on Baghdad,

30
00:02:24,780 --> 00:02:30,920
that CNN is transmitting, you know that someone in the Kremlin is watching the same picture.

31
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:34,980
Although newspapers have never been unbiased,

32
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:41,100
the expectation that they give the public a reliable report about what is happening in the world

33
00:02:41,101 --> 00:02:47,020
has afforded the press special protections and privileges not extended to other businesses.

34
00:02:48,260 --> 00:02:53,160
In our society, there is no other institution except for the press

35
00:02:53,161 --> 00:02:58,720
that really can make a president accountable, can question a president.

36
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:01,820
If you can't question a president, he can be a dictator.

37
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:04,260
He can rule by edict, by order.

38
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:10,100
So I really put reporters on a high plane in our society

39
00:03:10,101 --> 00:03:13,080
because that's what makes a democracy.

40
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:18,600
In following a story, reporters rely on their own observation,

41
00:03:19,580 --> 00:03:22,760
interviews with sources, and public records.

42
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:28,060
Even so, many claim that arriving at the truth is a challenge.

43
00:03:29,460 --> 00:03:32,780
I think that news has always been managed,

44
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:35,620
certainly since I've been covering the White House.

45
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:39,400
Managed, manipulated, controlled.

46
00:03:39,401 --> 00:03:42,900
but I think that's always been true in our country.

47
00:03:44,140 --> 00:03:48,920
There have been reports that you were told directly or indirectly, at least twice,

48
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:52,500
that the Contras were benefiting from the Iran arms sale.

49
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:55,300
Is that true? And I'd like to follow on.

50
00:03:55,800 --> 00:03:59,080
Helen, let me just say, no, that is not true at all.

51
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:02,460
It became a state of the art in the Reagan era.

52
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:05,500
We get spin every day here.

53
00:04:05,501 --> 00:04:06,400
Thank you.

54
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:11,160
Quindexter, did they deceive you?

55
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:15,400
You didn't answer whether Quindexter and North deceived you.

56
00:04:15,960 --> 00:04:19,320
Every president I've covered has tried to manage the news

57
00:04:19,321 --> 00:04:23,280
and control the output and the outlook.

58
00:04:23,940 --> 00:04:27,000
The process of shaping a story is one of give and take

59
00:04:27,001 --> 00:04:29,260
between a reporter and her editor.

60
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:33,260
Always they are on a race to meet the deadline.

61
00:04:33,820 --> 00:04:34,520
Okay.

62
00:04:34,521 --> 00:04:36,100
We'll, you know, in an hour and a half,

63
00:04:36,180 --> 00:04:37,740
we'll lose the ability to report it.

64
00:04:37,741 --> 00:04:42,300
So let's just grab what we can and then we'll slow down.

65
00:04:42,301 --> 00:04:46,600
We're driven by getting things quickly.

66
00:04:47,860 --> 00:04:55,920
And in the search to get there first, sometimes the truth is sacrificed.

67
00:04:57,120 --> 00:05:04,720
And we have to keep going back to what my assistant city editor told me

68
00:05:04,721 --> 00:05:08,600
when I was a copy boy on the verge of becoming a reporter,

69
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:10,960
get it first, but first get it right.

70
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:15,140
Reporters work on video display terminals

71
00:05:15,141 --> 00:05:17,540
connected to the paper's main computer.

72
00:05:18,660 --> 00:05:21,640
Their stories go directly to a copy desk for editing.

73
00:05:24,140 --> 00:05:28,020
The Graphic Arts Department supplies photos and illustrations.

74
00:05:28,021 --> 00:05:36,300
Some photos are shot by staff photographers.

75
00:05:36,301 --> 00:05:42,660
Others are retrieved from an Associated Press database that contains over 500 images and

76
00:05:42,661 --> 00:05:50,260
is updated every 24 hours.

77
00:05:50,261 --> 00:05:55,020
Based on the contents of that day's news, the design director works on the look of the

78
00:05:55,021 --> 00:06:02,020
front page, keeping in mind the specific criteria of his paper.

79
00:06:02,220 --> 00:06:07,720
We're an afternoon paper and we sell an inordinate amount of newspapers, single copy, on the

80
00:06:07,721 --> 00:06:13,800
street so we really have to have a front page and a whole newspaper that attracts people's

81
00:06:13,801 --> 00:06:18,741
attention and makes them want to put their quarter into the box each and every day.

82
00:06:18,742 --> 00:06:19,442
So

83
00:06:19,443 --> 00:06:22,180
we run large headlines.

84
00:06:22,181 --> 00:06:27,460
The last person to see the paper before it goes to press is the news editor.

85
00:06:27,461 --> 00:06:34,780
He is the man who must catch the mistakes before they happen.

86
00:06:34,781 --> 00:06:38,940
Grids holding the dummied up type are sent to the engraving department.

87
00:06:38,941 --> 00:06:45,140
Here stories, photos and ads are placed on a piece of waxed photographic paper, the exact

88
00:06:45,141 --> 00:06:49,420
size of a newspaper page.

89
00:06:49,421 --> 00:06:54,320
The laser reader scans the pages and transmits their image via microwave to the printing

90
00:06:54,321 --> 00:06:59,820
plant five miles away.

91
00:06:59,821 --> 00:07:05,860
Laser writing machines transfer the image of the page onto a piece of film.

92
00:07:05,861 --> 00:07:13,240
A technician places a thin plate of polymer plastic onto the negative of the film.

93
00:07:13,241 --> 00:07:18,540
A machine washes water over the plate, leaving a raised image of the page on the surface

94
00:07:18,541 --> 00:07:27,000
of the plastic.

95
00:07:27,001 --> 00:07:32,920
Workers wrap the plates around cylinders, one plate per page. Each cylinder holds a

96
00:07:32,921 --> 00:07:35,192
total of 16 plates.

97
00:07:35,193 --> 00:07:43,920
Robots transport 1,700-pound rolls of newsprint, seven miles long, to the

98
00:07:43,921 --> 00:07:44,621
press.

99
00:07:44,622 --> 00:07:52,260
As the paper is fed through the press, pages are automatically cut, folded, and collated

100
00:07:52,261 --> 00:07:54,498
into separate newspapers.

101
00:07:54,499 --> 00:07:59,960
Although each step happens at top speed, reporters admit they

102
00:07:59,961 --> 00:08:04,720
are no longer in the business of breaking news, whether they work for the Examiner or

103
00:08:04,721 --> 00:08:06,620
any of the morning newspapers.

104
00:08:06,621 --> 00:08:17,420
By the time a reader of our newspaper picks it up off the doorstep at 7 o'clock the morning after I've written the story,

105
00:08:18,140 --> 00:08:26,700
he's seen the fact that there has been bombing in Bosnia or that there is an economic meltdown in Russia.

106
00:08:27,260 --> 00:08:32,220
He's heard that on the evening news and he's heard it on breakfast time.

107
00:08:32,221 --> 00:08:39,220
Editors believe that newspapers should fulfill a broader function.

108
00:08:39,221 --> 00:08:45,220
Our responsibility, in a sense, is to present our readers with things that they don't get anywhere else.

109
00:08:45,221 --> 00:08:50,220
So it's not simply to inform or to engage in public service, which we also should do,

110
00:08:50,221 --> 00:08:55,220
but it's also to amuse, to entertain, to shock, to outrage.

111
00:08:55,221 --> 00:09:01,220
Indeed, the history of the newspaper itself is an emotionally charged story.

112
00:09:02,220 --> 00:09:05,380
from the propaganda pamphlets of the Revolutionary War

113
00:09:05,381 --> 00:09:09,380
to the censorship of the socialist press during World War One.

114
00:09:20,220 --> 00:09:23,420
The death of a king, danger to the nation,

115
00:09:23,421 --> 00:09:28,860
or the emergence of a new leader have always been considered newsworthy events.

116
00:09:28,861 --> 00:09:37,720
In the ancient world, as through much of human history, such news was delivered by the only means available, word of mouth.

117
00:09:39,080 --> 00:09:44,980
From even the earliest times, a high premium was placed on the speed and accuracy of the news.

118
00:09:46,180 --> 00:09:54,440
Messengers were trained to heighten their physical endurance and to sharpen their memory by rhyming phrases into long epics like the Iliad.

119
00:09:54,441 --> 00:10:01,320
One of the first improvements on spoken news occurred in ancient Rome

120
00:10:01,321 --> 00:10:04,840
when Julius Caesar initiated a daily news sheet

121
00:10:04,841 --> 00:10:07,320
with reports on the Roman Senate

122
00:10:07,321 --> 00:10:15,520
During the Middle Ages, monks and scribes laboriously reproduced manuscripts by hand

123
00:10:15,521 --> 00:10:21,720
Then in the 1440s in Mainz, Germany

124
00:10:21,721 --> 00:10:25,680
Johann Gutenberg began experimenting with movable type.

125
00:10:26,680 --> 00:10:29,420
By casting individual letters out of metal,

126
00:10:29,920 --> 00:10:34,860
he created pieces of type that could be combined and recombined over and over again.

127
00:10:38,080 --> 00:10:43,180
In a process that took two years, he set and printed an edition of the Bible.

128
00:10:44,880 --> 00:10:48,840
Gutenberg's invention forever changed the course of human history.

129
00:10:48,841 --> 00:10:55,340
Its full impact made itself felt 50 years later in 1493

130
00:10:55,341 --> 00:11:02,260
When a press reproduced a thousand copies of a letter written by Christopher Columbus to his queen

131
00:11:02,261 --> 00:11:05,960
Announcing the discovery of a new world

132
00:11:05,961 --> 00:11:09,620
The newspaper was on its way

133
00:11:11,200 --> 00:11:15,720
But by modern day standards, information moved at a snail's pace

134
00:11:15,721 --> 00:11:22,120
In colonial America, newly arrived settlers waited for months for news from home traveling by ship.

135
00:11:23,400 --> 00:11:27,400
The earliest efforts at reporting, actually, in the United States

136
00:11:27,401 --> 00:11:32,980
were simply printers walking down to the docks and trying to get the news a little bit quicker

137
00:11:32,981 --> 00:11:34,900
so they could put it into their newspaper.

138
00:11:34,901 --> 00:11:38,040
Then somebody got even more industrious, and they got a rowboat

139
00:11:38,041 --> 00:11:40,600
and rowed out and met the ships a little bit earlier.

140
00:11:41,980 --> 00:11:44,900
Newspapers were a spirited voice in colonial society.

141
00:11:45,720 --> 00:11:50,720
After the British Crown imposed a harsh stamp tax on the sale of newspapers,

142
00:11:50,721 --> 00:11:54,720
publishers like Benjamin Franklin used bold graphics

143
00:11:54,721 --> 00:11:58,720
to trumpet the cause of independence

144
00:11:58,721 --> 00:12:03,720
and became a major force in fueling the Revolutionary War.

145
00:12:03,721 --> 00:12:06,720
These papers were not objective, they were not fair.

146
00:12:06,721 --> 00:12:11,720
They chose sides and they fought for that side and they helped make a revolution by doing that.

147
00:12:11,721 --> 00:12:18,720
At the beginning of the 19th century, news was still traveling only as fast as men could carry it.

148
00:12:18,721 --> 00:12:24,720
One of the great stories of the slowness of news is the War of 1812.

149
00:12:24,721 --> 00:12:28,720
The War of 1812 ended, this was a peace treaty signed in Europe,

150
00:12:28,721 --> 00:12:32,720
but they didn't find out about it in the United States for many weeks after that,

151
00:12:32,721 --> 00:12:38,720
so the biggest battle of the war, the Battle of New Orleans, was fought after the war ended.

152
00:12:38,721 --> 00:12:45,020
During the early 19th century, papers were printed on a flatbed press.

153
00:12:45,680 --> 00:12:48,300
Printers operated the heavy machinery by hand.

154
00:12:49,100 --> 00:12:51,700
The process was slow and expensive.

155
00:12:53,220 --> 00:12:56,100
The newspaper had pretty much a rich audience.

156
00:12:56,320 --> 00:12:59,020
It was expensive to buy. It cost six cents a copy,

157
00:12:59,200 --> 00:13:04,000
which was a significant percentage of a working person's salary in those days.

158
00:13:04,300 --> 00:13:06,660
So working people really weren't reading newspapers.

159
00:13:06,661 --> 00:13:13,320
In the 1830s, the newly invented steam engine was harnessed to power the press,

160
00:13:13,800 --> 00:13:16,780
and suddenly thousands of copies could be reproduced.

161
00:13:18,080 --> 00:13:24,500
At the same time, the manufacture of cheaper paper and the rise of urban transportation systems

162
00:13:24,501 --> 00:13:29,540
enabled publishers to cut the price of their newspapers to a penny a copy.

163
00:13:29,541 --> 00:13:39,540
But it was not until the 1840s that the news business would be truly transformed by a new invention, the telegraph.

164
00:13:39,541 --> 00:13:49,563
The telegraph, of course, brought faster news and it allowed news organizations, newspapers all over the country to have information transmitted by correspondents in

165
00:13:49,564 --> 00:13:52,540
the field instantaneously across telegraph wires.

166
00:13:52,541 --> 00:13:58,180
telegraph wires. The telegraph played a crucial part in perhaps the biggest news

167
00:13:58,181 --> 00:14:04,540
story to ever hit the United States. The Civil War. It was a story that people

168
00:14:04,541 --> 00:14:09,820
most wanted and needed to know about. So during the Civil War the success of your

169
00:14:09,821 --> 00:14:14,500
newspaper depended on your ability to get reports of battles back as quickly as

170
00:14:14,501 --> 00:14:20,260
possible. By its very nature the mechanics of transmitting by telegraph

171
00:14:20,261 --> 00:14:22,860
dictated the manner in which stories were written.

172
00:14:24,200 --> 00:14:27,000
It was very expensive to transmit this information.

173
00:14:27,460 --> 00:14:29,460
We got a change in style of newspapers.

174
00:14:29,600 --> 00:14:32,520
Newspapers went from being long, elegant essays

175
00:14:32,521 --> 00:14:34,700
to short, truncated reports.

176
00:14:39,120 --> 00:14:42,020
And comparable to the writing of the present day

177
00:14:42,021 --> 00:14:44,900
with a short lead and with the movement eventually

178
00:14:44,901 --> 00:14:46,820
from the most important item first

179
00:14:46,821 --> 00:14:49,820
and then in descending order the rest of the information.

180
00:14:50,260 --> 00:14:58,260
Newspaper publishing was becoming an important industry and New York City its center.

181
00:14:58,261 --> 00:15:04,260
In the 1880s a German immigrant named Joseph Pulitzer established the world,

182
00:15:04,261 --> 00:15:11,260
hired a few top journalists and graphic designers and turned news into entertainment.

183
00:15:11,261 --> 00:15:16,260
There was a revolution in graphics and design and display.

184
00:15:16,261 --> 00:15:23,540
and display they brought in a sense of sensationalism and a sense of excitement and a spirited notion

185
00:15:23,541 --> 00:15:31,140
of journalism the press went from being kind of the gray lady to bold brassy headlines

186
00:15:32,980 --> 00:15:40,420
by mixing sensationalism and entertainment bullitzer created yellow journalism and built an empire

187
00:15:40,421 --> 00:15:48,060
It was not long before a transplant from California, William Randolph Hearst entered the scene.

188
00:15:49,220 --> 00:15:55,760
A 33-year-old Hearst purchased the New York Journal in hopes of stealing a piece of Pulitzer's market share.

189
00:15:58,240 --> 00:16:07,520
His first coup was the reporting of a seemingly obscure incident, 50 miles off the coast of Florida on the Spanish-owned island of Cuba.

190
00:16:07,521 --> 00:16:15,400
first heard a story of a young cuban woman who was arrested and placed in a spanish jail

191
00:16:15,401 --> 00:16:22,380
in cuba and boy did he run with this story he sent a reporter down to rescue her and he got

192
00:16:22,381 --> 00:16:27,300
this woman out of jail and brought her back to new york and paraded her through the streets of

193
00:16:27,301 --> 00:16:33,680
new york as a great heroine the following week an unexplained explosion in havana's harbor

194
00:16:33,681 --> 00:16:37,160
in which the American battleship the Maine was destroyed

195
00:16:37,161 --> 00:16:41,760
gave Hearst the excuse he needed to promote war with Spain.

196
00:16:43,900 --> 00:16:48,000
Indeed, the notion of you provide the pictures, I'll provide the war

197
00:16:48,001 --> 00:16:51,780
was a message supposedly from Hearst

198
00:16:51,781 --> 00:16:55,160
to his editors and correspondents in the field in Cuba.

199
00:16:56,540 --> 00:16:59,160
From the point of view of newspaper publishers,

200
00:16:59,860 --> 00:17:02,640
the Spanish-American War was a huge success.

201
00:17:03,680 --> 00:17:17,409
The correspondents, many of whom had helped really start the war by fanning anti-Spanish sentiment in this country, were all over the place, covering things like Teddy

202
00:17:17,410 --> 00:17:22,260
It was a nice short war. Roosevelt's ride up San Juan Hill.

203
00:17:25,240 --> 00:17:31,140
Six years later, Roosevelt's well-publicized heroics won him the presidency.

204
00:17:33,680 --> 00:17:40,100
The fierce competition between Dailies forced publishers to resort to theatrics to sell papers.

205
00:17:41,640 --> 00:17:47,900
In one of the most widely publicized of these escapades, Nellie Bly, a New York world reporter,

206
00:17:48,380 --> 00:17:55,400
set out to challenge Jules Verne's fictional account by going around the world in less than 80 days.

207
00:17:57,060 --> 00:18:00,291
People were back home rooting for her. Would Nellie make it?

208
00:18:00,292 --> 00:18:01,960
They were taking wagers on her.

209
00:18:01,961 --> 00:18:07,180
These were the days of stunt journalism, when James Gordon Bennett Jr.

210
00:18:07,181 --> 00:18:11,580
sent Henry Morton Stanley to find Dr. Livingston in Africa.

211
00:18:12,080 --> 00:18:13,497
And the famous quote, Dr.

212
00:18:13,498 --> 00:18:17,180
Livingston, I presume, comes from a story in the New York Herald.

213
00:18:17,600 --> 00:18:24,700
People who themselves really weren't traveling much in these days lived vicariously through some of these newspaper adventures.

214
00:18:24,701 --> 00:18:31,700
The circulation of the Pulitzer and Hearst dailies each exceeded more than one million readers.

215
00:18:31,701 --> 00:18:37,700
Throughout the country newspaper barons amassed such astounding fortunes

216
00:18:37,701 --> 00:18:41,700
that many entered the highest ranks of the Fortune 500.

217
00:18:41,701 --> 00:18:49,700
No one suspected that the future of the newspaper might soon be in jeopardy.

218
00:18:54,700 --> 00:19:02,460
During the first decade of the new century,

219
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,600
newspapers reached the zenith of their power.

220
00:19:06,540 --> 00:19:09,540
They were the fastest and most reliable way to get the news,

221
00:19:10,220 --> 00:19:13,540
with extra editions published up to three times a day.

222
00:19:15,920 --> 00:19:18,380
Intrepid journalists searched for new beats

223
00:19:18,381 --> 00:19:20,540
that would give them an edge over competitors.

224
00:19:21,620 --> 00:19:23,620
During Teddy Roosevelt's administration,

225
00:19:23,621 --> 00:19:27,680
one reporter even camped outside the gates of the White House.

226
00:19:30,160 --> 00:19:34,900
Pretty soon he was nabbing everyone who would come in to see President Roosevelt

227
00:19:34,901 --> 00:19:39,400
and was getting great stories about Teddy and so forth.

228
00:19:39,620 --> 00:19:45,400
And after that, other reporters around town thought that must be the best beat in town.

229
00:19:46,160 --> 00:19:49,700
And pretty soon when it rained and so forth,

230
00:19:49,701 --> 00:19:56,340
