and print broadcasters may be more capable of doing that than broadcasters.

464
00:39:25,440 --> 00:39:30,700
For those working at the Media Lab at MIT, the future is here.

465
00:39:30,701 --> 00:39:39,180
If you have an electronic form, you can add additional news for any individual without that additional cost.

466
00:39:39,900 --> 00:39:47,700
So suddenly there's an economic that can support my in-depth interest on a particular subject like bicycling,

467
00:39:49,020 --> 00:39:58,700
where I can get much, much more news about bicycling than the Boston Globe would ever think was useful or economical or practical to print in the paper.

468
00:39:58,701 --> 00:40:06,700
Today, news delivered via the Internet can be personalized to suit the needs of an individual.

469
00:40:06,701 --> 00:40:13,700
Many, many websites, for example, that have the word my in front of them.

470
00:40:13,701 --> 00:40:16,700
They give you my news, my Yahoo, my Excite, etc.

471
00:40:16,701 --> 00:40:22,700
And what those websites allow you to do is they allow you to specify topics of interest,

472
00:40:22,701 --> 00:40:29,320
So you'll get additional information about those topics on a daily basis or whatever clock cycle you choose.

473
00:40:30,860 --> 00:40:35,440
Professional journalists also view the Internet as an invaluable asset.

474
00:40:36,600 --> 00:40:44,420
The challenge for a reporter today is using the computer as a full tool and using the Internet.

475
00:40:44,640 --> 00:40:46,900
The Internet's a terrific news source.

476
00:40:46,901 --> 00:40:55,620
A Pentagon correspondent discovered military chat rooms where soldiers were kind of talking back and forth.

477
00:40:55,780 --> 00:40:56,900
You're a fly on the wall.

478
00:40:57,880 --> 00:40:59,580
And you can pick up stories.

479
00:41:00,360 --> 00:41:01,880
It's a tremendous archive.

480
00:41:02,140 --> 00:41:07,120
The Internet is just this wealth of information for you as much as it is for me.

481
00:41:07,260 --> 00:41:09,260
But for me, information is my business.

482
00:41:10,460 --> 00:41:14,800
For those comfortable with the capabilities of digital communication,

483
00:41:14,801 --> 00:41:17,360
the possibilities are boundless.

484
00:41:18,080 --> 00:41:21,840
For instance, a newspaper published on a desktop computer

485
00:41:21,841 --> 00:41:25,020
can now be disseminated over the World Wide Web.

486
00:41:26,460 --> 00:41:31,900
We gave children the same tools and allowed them to create a newspaper.

487
00:41:32,440 --> 00:41:37,000
And we've got a group of about 3,000 children from around the world.

488
00:41:37,280 --> 00:41:39,460
There are 139 countries represented.

489
00:41:39,880 --> 00:41:43,860
And they're publishing an online publication called the Junior Journal.

490
00:41:43,861 --> 00:41:53,100
And that newspaper is their expression about what it means to be a kid in various societies around the world.

491
00:41:54,220 --> 00:42:02,160
Experts believe that digital news publication has the power to transform news organizations in a positive sense.

492
00:42:03,860 --> 00:42:11,780
I recollect that probably close to 50% of the cost of a newspaper has nothing to do with content.

493
00:42:11,781 --> 00:42:13,900
it has to do with delivery of that content.

494
00:42:14,120 --> 00:42:16,760
So the cost of paper, the cost of ink,

495
00:42:16,880 --> 00:42:18,300
the cost of operating the presses,

496
00:42:18,740 --> 00:42:20,660
the cost of running the trucks for delivery.

497
00:42:21,520 --> 00:42:25,000
That's a lot of the expense of newspapers.

498
00:42:26,460 --> 00:42:29,240
And wouldn't it be nice if that expense

499
00:42:29,241 --> 00:42:33,280
could be turned into more news?

500
00:42:34,740 --> 00:42:37,020
News delivery over the Internet

501
00:42:37,021 --> 00:42:40,220
may change the way consumers think about news,

502
00:42:40,221 --> 00:42:43,780
encouraging them to take on a more active role.

503
00:42:44,820 --> 00:42:48,520
There's value here and it's not high tech.

504
00:42:48,660 --> 00:42:53,360
It's just really rethinking what are the roles that people play in news?

505
00:42:53,460 --> 00:42:55,560
What are the roles that people play in storytelling?

506
00:42:55,860 --> 00:42:59,120
It's not just a top-down thing anymore.

507
00:42:59,260 --> 00:43:01,880
It's not just a top-down medium, but it's also bottom-up.

508
00:43:03,120 --> 00:43:07,240
It seems clear that we are at the edge of a revolution in thinking.

509
00:43:07,241 --> 00:43:13,020
as the internet empowers its users with a new set of tools for exploring the world

510
00:43:13,021 --> 00:43:15,860
newspapers are scrambling to adjust

511
00:43:15,861 --> 00:43:21,740
chances are they will survive and even flourish

512
00:43:21,741 --> 00:43:25,980
for over the course of their 500 year long history

513
00:43:25,981 --> 00:43:31,060
they have proven themselves to be a remarkably resilient creation

514
00:43:37,240 --> 00:43:45,240
Cosmonaut or astronaut, Russian or American, the pre-launch preparations are much the same.

515
00:43:45,241 --> 00:43:52,240
Exercises and drills that discipline the mind and toughen the body for flights into space.

516
00:43:52,241 --> 00:43:59,240
It is for such a journey that a Russian spaceman trains in secret in the summer of 1962.

517
00:43:59,241 --> 00:44:12,520
His name is Andriy Nikolayev, and at a Russian Cosmodrome somewhere in Central Asia, he is ready on August 11 for blastoff.

518
00:44:12,521 --> 00:44:26,080
More than a million pounds of rocket thrust send the Soviet spacecraft spinning into an

519
00:44:26,081 --> 00:44:37,920
orbit that is 156 miles above the Earth at its peak, 113 miles at its low point.

520
00:44:37,921 --> 00:44:40,840
when cosmonaut Nikolaev is safely in orbit

521
00:44:40,841 --> 00:44:42,720
as the world learn of the launching.

522
00:44:45,740 --> 00:44:49,080
In Moscow, crowds in the streets hear radio reports

523
00:44:49,081 --> 00:44:51,440
from loudspeakers atop buildings in Red Square.

524
00:44:52,160 --> 00:44:54,840
It is a year and five days since Germantitov

525
00:44:54,841 --> 00:44:56,920
orbited the Earth 17 times.

526
00:44:57,480 --> 00:44:59,120
The Russian people and the free world

527
00:44:59,121 --> 00:45:01,040
listen, wait, and wonder.

528
00:45:01,680 --> 00:45:03,500
What is the real purpose and importance

529
00:45:03,501 --> 00:45:06,220
of the flight of cosmonaut Andrei Nikolaev?

530
00:45:07,920 --> 00:45:20,920
Into the night, the spaceship and its lonely riders circle the earth at 18,000 miles an hour, making one complete orbit every 88 minutes.

531
00:45:20,921 --> 00:45:33,440
Then the first in a series of spectacular space-age achievements.

532
00:45:34,220 --> 00:45:39,240
Television pictures of Nikolayev taken electronically in his capsule and relayed to Earth.

533
00:45:39,820 --> 00:45:42,880
Viewers see the cosmonaut attempt to tie down his logbook

534
00:45:42,881 --> 00:45:46,620
as it floats freely in the eerie world of zero gravity.

535
00:45:50,920 --> 00:45:59,800
Premier Khrushchev himself takes part in a second communications link between Earth and

536
00:45:59,801 --> 00:46:04,100
space, a direct telephone conversation between the Russian leader and Nikolaev.

537
00:46:04,101 --> 00:46:14,340
The Cosmonaut, who begins his broadcasts with a greeting, this is Falcon, reports all is

538
00:46:14,341 --> 00:46:16,100
well with himself and his spaceship.

539
00:46:16,580 --> 00:46:17,740
Khrushchev replies.

540
00:46:28,380 --> 00:46:30,380
I'm glad to hear that you are fine,

541
00:46:30,780 --> 00:46:31,660
says the Soviet premier,

542
00:46:32,180 --> 00:46:33,660
and I wish you a safe return.

543
00:46:38,620 --> 00:46:40,500
Top-ranking members of the Soviet

544
00:46:40,501 --> 00:46:42,440
Politburo are quick to see the

545
00:46:42,441 --> 00:46:46,540
propaganda significance of the Russian exploit. And led by Communist Party

546
00:46:46,541 --> 00:46:50,560
Secretary Frau Kozlov and Deputy Premier Anastas Mikoyan,

547
00:46:50,940 --> 00:46:54,060
they know that there is still another space conquest to come.

548
00:47:06,200 --> 00:47:07,960
A second cosmonaut,

549
00:47:07,961 --> 00:47:12,960
Pavel Popovich is hurled into orbit 24 hours after Nikolaev.

550
00:47:12,961 --> 00:47:18,960
Together they will circle the Earth for three days, at one time only three miles apart.

551
00:47:18,961 --> 00:47:28,960
Each will travel more than a million miles in space, ending an historic journey that has taken them out of this world and back by parachuting safely to Earth.

552
00:47:28,961 --> 00:47:38,960
Four days after landing, the cosmonauts return to Moscow and the acclaim of their countrymen.

553
00:47:38,961 --> 00:47:44,960
The 32-year-old Nikolai is on the right, the 31-year-old Popovich at his side, as Russia

554
00:47:44,961 --> 00:47:49,960
rolls out a red carpet 100 feet long to welcome them home.

555
00:47:58,960 --> 00:48:03,960
The two men mount the receiving stand and salute as one.

556
00:48:03,961 --> 00:48:10,960
Never before has anyone, even a visiting head of state, received a more spectacular and propaganda conscious greeting.

557
00:48:10,961 --> 00:48:15,960
Nikolaev is embraced and kissed repeatedly by Prima Kruschev.

558
00:48:19,960 --> 00:48:23,960
Next, Bafovic receives his welcome from the jubilant Kruschev.

559
00:48:28,960 --> 00:48:35,960
Then a twenty mile trip to Red Square, with Nikolayev at left and Popovich riding in an open car.

560
00:48:41,660 --> 00:48:49,660
Here atop Lenin's tomb, the Heavenly Twins, as the Soviet newspapers call them, receive the salute of 80,000 Russians.

561
00:48:49,661 --> 00:49:00,560
The rest of the world joins too in applauding another milestone on mankind's journey to the stars.

562
00:49:01,660 --> 00:49:09,720
In America, there is no secrecy, no iron curtain, as work is completed on a jewel-encrusted satellite called Telstar,

563
00:49:10,200 --> 00:49:14,800
designed to span oceans and link continents through a worldwide television hookup.

564
00:49:19,660 --> 00:49:25,940
Three feet in diameter, weighing 170 pounds, Telstar is a relay station in space.

565
00:49:25,941 --> 00:49:31,780
It is crammed with 15,000 intricate parts whose purpose is to receive signals from Earth,

566
00:49:32,340 --> 00:49:36,620
amplify them 10 billion times, and transmit them back to the ground.

567
00:49:39,740 --> 00:49:44,540
The satellite marks the first time a private company has built a space vehicle

568
00:49:44,541 --> 00:49:47,480
and paid the cost of launching from its own funds.

569
00:49:49,660 --> 00:50:02,290
The most important of all, Telstar symbolizes space exploration as the peaceful servant of mankind, bringing faraway peoples, places, and events into the living room

570
00:50:02,291 --> 00:50:04,740
of any home, any time, anywhere.

571
00:50:06,480 --> 00:50:15,660
And so in the free and open and democratic way, all the world knows and waits as the countdown for Project Telstar gets underway at Cape Canaveral.

572
00:50:15,661 --> 00:50:25,660
The little communications satellite is tucked in the nose of a Delta rocket that towers 90 feet above the launching pan.

573
00:50:25,661 --> 00:50:34,660
Forty-five million dollars in research and development, tens of thousands of man-hours.

574
00:50:34,661 --> 00:50:36,316
This is what they lead to.

575
00:50:36,317 --> 00:50:44,660
This is where they end, in the final, fateful seconds that are, for the launch crew and the rocket, the inevitable moment of truth.

576
00:51:14,660 --> 00:51:28,660
All eyes follow the rocket's red glow, waiting for the blinding burst of flame that will signal the igniting of the second stage.

577
00:51:34,040 --> 00:51:45,120
11 minutes after launching, Telstar is in orbit, circling the Earth every 160 minutes

578
00:51:45,121 --> 00:51:50,880
Ground stations make contact, and the satellite responds

579
00:51:50,881 --> 00:51:58,420
It relays not only television signals, but telephone calls, still pictures, and teletype messages

580
00:51:58,421 --> 00:52:07,420
Before Telstar, relaying a live transatlantic television signal would have required a mid-ocean tower at the impossible height of 150 miles.

581
00:52:07,421 --> 00:52:14,420
Now TV stations can reach out beyond the horizon, around the curve of the Earth.

582
00:52:14,421 --> 00:52:22,420
Already special telecasts have crossed the Atlantic, America to Europe, Europe to America,

583
00:52:22,421 --> 00:52:27,740
America, heralding the day when 30 or more tell stars will girdle the earth in a global

584
00:52:27,741 --> 00:52:34,080
network, helping nations find among themselves a common understanding, and in that understanding

585
00:52:34,081 --> 00:52:35,960
a brighter hope for peace.

586
00:52:36,820 --> 00:52:44,260
In this search is remembered the words spoken 100 years ago by Abraham Lincoln, words about

587
00:52:44,261 --> 00:52:48,560
a divided country that today have equal meaning to a divided planet.

588
00:52:48,561 --> 00:52:54,060
The dogmas of the quiet past are inadequate to the stormy present.

589
00:52:54,061 --> 00:53:00,500
As our case is new, so must we think anew and act anew.

590
00:53:33,317 --> 00:53:40,320
America, America, God shed His grace on thee, and crown my earth with brotherhood from sea to shining sea.

